WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:09.840
When Prince Louis of France invaded England in&nbsp;
1216, he met unexpected resistance deep in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.560
dense forests of southern England.&nbsp;
A man named William of Kensham,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.120
along with a group of partisans, struck&nbsp;
fear into the hearts of the French. Much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.920
like the legendary Robin Hood and his daring&nbsp;
companions, they lay in wait with their bows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:29.360
attacking French knights whenever they ventured&nbsp;
into the forest. Their weapons were cunning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:35.600
surprise, and terror. They would appear out of&nbsp;
nowhere, ambush a convoy, kill a few stragglers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:41.360
and vanish silently into the shadows of the woods.
William of Kensham and his men played a crucial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.000
role in repelling the French invasion&nbsp;
during the First Barons’ War. They are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:53.280
among England’s unsung heroes—admired by their&nbsp;
contemporaries but forgotten by history … almost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.210
that is. Perhaps part of their legacy&nbsp;
lives on in the legend of Robin Hood.&nbsp;

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:14.800
William of Kensham—The Real Robin Hood?
Our story takes us back to a turbulent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:21.520
chapter of England’s past. The country was ruled&nbsp;
by King John Lackland, best known as the villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:28.880
in the Robin Hood stories. In reality,&nbsp;
John was an incompetent tyrant. In 1215,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.480
his subjects forced him to sign the famous Magna&nbsp;
Carta, a document that outlined the rights and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.520
freedoms of the nobility in relation to the&nbsp;
king. But John had no intention of limiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:48.080
his power and soon had the Magna Carta declared&nbsp;
invalid by the Pope. This sparked a civil war.&nbsp;

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.440
Outnumbered by the king’s mercenary armies, the&nbsp;
rebels turned to Prince Louis of France for help.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.800
Louis crossed the English Channel with an&nbsp;
invasion army in May, and within a few months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.720
he had occupied almost a third of England.&nbsp;
The nobles in this area faced a grim choice:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:10.640
join Louis or flee, leaving their subjects&nbsp;
at the mercy of the ruthless French.&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:16.160
While Louis worked to clear the last pockets&nbsp;
of resistance—most notably the mighty castle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:21.680
of Dover—a common man named William gathered&nbsp;
the bravest of these abandoned people around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:28.720
him in the depths of the forest. Far behind&nbsp;
enemy lines, Willikin of the Weald, as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:35.840
soon came to be known, prepared for a guerrilla&nbsp;
war that would earn him fame throughout England.&nbsp;

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.320
Little is known about William’s life&nbsp;
before the war. According to the chronicler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:48.320
Roger of Wendover, he was a young man, probably&nbsp;
around 20 to 25 years old. He was likely not from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:54.240
the area. Historian Sean McGlynn suggests that&nbsp;
William may have come to England in 1215 with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.840
the mercenaries King John brought from Flanders&nbsp;
to defend himself against rebellious subjects.&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.920
He did not take the nickname Kensham—or&nbsp;
Cassingham—until later, when he settled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:12.080
on the estate of the same name, located between&nbsp;
Sandhurst and Rolvenden in Kent. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.640
he must have spent some time in southern England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:21.040
as he was already very familiar with the&nbsp;
so-called Weald when the invasion began.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:26.320
The Weald is an extensive forested region&nbsp;
stretching across Kent, Sussex, and Surrey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:33.360
to Hampshire. In the Middle Ages, it was a rugged&nbsp;
and inaccessible area that served as a refuge for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:40.320
many outlaws. Winding paths led through dense&nbsp;
undergrowth, over deep gorges and rocky hills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.760
with only a few isolated farmsteads&nbsp;
scattered along the way. For an army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:52.960
the Weald was virtually impenetrable—but&nbsp;
for a partisan like William, it was perfect.&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:59.040
According to Roger of Wendover, William quickly&nbsp;
gathered “one thousand archers and took to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:05.760
isolated places and forests which abounded in&nbsp;
that region.” While the “one thousand” number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.480
should not be taken literally, William’s&nbsp;
forces still amounted to a small army by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:15.360
the standards of the time—an impressive&nbsp;
feat, since even wealthy landowners could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:20.080
rarely raise troops on that scale.
However, these partisans were not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:26.240
professional soldiers. A few may have had prior&nbsp;
combat experience and could therefore prepare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.280
their comrades for the challenges ahead, but&nbsp;
most were simple men from the countryside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:36.880
armed with nothing more than the bows&nbsp;
and arrows they used in everyday life.&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:42.000
To prevail against the heavily armored French&nbsp;
soldiers, William and his men relied on a tactic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.080
used successfully by partisans throughout history.&nbsp;
They followed the principles of guerrilla warfare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:54.800
striking the invaders in many small, secretive&nbsp;
actions. “All the time,” reports Wendover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.520
“they attacked and disrupted the enemy, and&nbsp;
as a result of their intense resistance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:05.600
many thousands of Frenchmen were slain.”
Their greatest advantage was their intimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.960
knowledge of the Weald. Many were born and raised&nbsp;
there, and could rely on a network of friends and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:18.400
family who secretly supported them. According to&nbsp;
Sean McGlynn, some may even have led double lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:24.720
fighting the English from the comfort of their own&nbsp;
homes. These men were at home in the forest—they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:30.800
knew exactly where to hide, which winding paths to&nbsp;
take to shake off pursuers, and where to set traps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:36.240
for their enemies. In open battle, they would&nbsp;
have stood little chance against Louis’s men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:42.640
but in the forest, they were vastly superior.
We know very little about exactly how William&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:47.120
operated. This is partly because he&nbsp;
remained as secretive as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:52.800
and partly because the reports of his fighting&nbsp;
lack sufficient detail. It is worth remembering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.920
that this was a time when it was not always&nbsp;
possible to know the whereabouts of a king,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:04.240
let alone a common man hiding in the forest.
What we do know is that William’s men relied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:11.840
on the methods of so-called small war. Their most&nbsp;
effective weapons were ambushes, raids, and fear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:17.600
They moved in small, highly mobile groups, lightly&nbsp;
armed, and almost impossible for the French scouts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:24.720
to find, let alone catch. Often, they avoided&nbsp;
direct confrontations, instead crippling enemy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:30.880
communications and supply lines, picking off&nbsp;
stragglers, or setting traps for the French.&nbsp;

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:35.440
The bow was the perfect weapon for this&nbsp;
kind of warfare. Light and flexible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:40.320
it allowed the partisans to move faster than&nbsp;
Louis’s heavily armored troops. They roamed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:46.000
forests like the wind, while French infantry&nbsp;
repeatedly got stuck in the undergrowth—and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:51.600
that’s not even to mention the men-at-arms.
With their bows, the partisans could strike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:57.680
silently from a distance while keeping themselves&nbsp;
out of danger. Their targets often didn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:03.600
notice them until they heard the whizz of an&nbsp;
arrow and someone dropped to the ground. By the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.800
time they recovered from the shock, the archers&nbsp;
had long since vanished into the dense forest.&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:15.760
It was this very unpredictability that struck&nbsp;
fear into the hearts of the French. They knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:20.720
that no mercy awaited them, making them&nbsp;
all the more reluctant to enter the dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:26.160
unfamiliar forests. William exploited&nbsp;
this fear to the fullest. Operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:31.520
far behind enemy lines in French-controlled&nbsp;
territory with virtually no infrastructure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:37.680
he turned the forest into his fortress and&nbsp;
fear into his weapon. Anyone who fell into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:39.680
his hands was mercilessly executed,&nbsp;
usually by having his head cut off.&nbsp;

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:48.320
William and his partisans were so successful that&nbsp;
even King John took notice. In September 1216, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:54.080
king personally thanked William in a letter for&nbsp;
his support and soon afterward sent funds to aid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.080 --> 00:08:01.120
the resistance in the Weald. When John Lackland&nbsp;
died in October 1216 and his nine-year-old son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:08.640
Henry succeeded him to the throne William remained&nbsp;
loyal to the crown. If you want to understand more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:14.160
about the political backdrop to this time period,&nbsp;
the rule of the Royal House of the Plantagenets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:19.840
we can't recommend Sharon Kay Penman's Plantagenet&nbsp;
Series enough. To be clear: this is historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:24.480
fiction but it’s well researched and gives&nbsp;
you a good idea about the time period leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:29.440
up to John Lackland’s reign. Now, we love&nbsp;
sinking into historical fiction on the go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:33.680
which is why we’ve been dedicated Audible users&nbsp;
for six years. If you want to start listening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:38.080
to the Plantagenet Series, you can actually get&nbsp;
a free book and support our channel at the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:43.280
time by signing up for an Audible trial using&nbsp;
our affiliate link. It costs you nothing extra,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:48.400
and when you sign up, we get a small compensation.&nbsp;
Thanks for considering it, and now, let's get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:53.440
back to the true history of Robin Hood.
. The king’s death fundamentally changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:59.280
the situation in England. Suddenly, the main&nbsp;
cause of the nobles’ discontent had vanished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:05.040
and they realized their rebellion had opened the&nbsp;
door to a French invasion. Many abandoned the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:11.680
rebellion and pledged allegiance to Henry III,&nbsp;
making Louis’s position far more precarious.&nbsp;

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:18.720
By early 1217, Louis had run out of money and&nbsp;
troops. He was forced to agree to a truce with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:24.240
the English, break off the siege of Dover&nbsp;
Castle, and return to London. As he made his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:29.920
way south to fetch reinforcements from France&nbsp;
and replenish his war chest, the moment was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:37.840
ripe for William’s masterstroke. He set a trap&nbsp;
for Louis that nearly cost him his entire army.&nbsp;

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:43.840
William’s partisans were already on Louis’s&nbsp;
heels as he marched through Kent and Sussex.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:48.160
They prowled the woods along the roads,&nbsp;
systematically destroying anything edible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:53.760
in the French army’s path and capturing men&nbsp;
who strayed too far from the marching column.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:10:00.080
By the time Louis finally left the Weald&nbsp;
behind, his troops were starving, exhausted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:06.480
and demoralized—just as William had planned.&nbsp;
They were now vulnerable enough for an ambush.&nbsp;

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:13.680
When the French army caught sight of Lewes, they&nbsp;
longed for a restful night behind secure walls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:17.920
But just before they reached the town,&nbsp;
the bushes and hills on either side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:24.240
the road suddenly sprang to life. Arrows&nbsp;
and stones rained down like thick hail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:30.320
knocking down dozens of unprepared soldiers. With&nbsp;
great difficulty, Louis managed to regroup his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:35.840
troops and fight his way out of the ambush.&nbsp;
In a rush, he marched east along the coast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:42.640
intending to seek shelter in Winchelsea and wait&nbsp;
for the arrival of his fleet. The partisans stayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:49.920
close behind, allowing him not a moment’s rest.
Louis found Winchelsea deserted. The townspeople&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:56.080
were gone, and the storehouses had been emptied.&nbsp;
He immediately sent messengers to London to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:02.000
request reinforcements and supplies—but&nbsp;
none returned. Every one of the men he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:08.800
sent was blocked by William, who had surrounded&nbsp;
Winchelsea with a tight cordon. His partisans had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:14.560
destroyed bridges behind the French, blocked&nbsp;
the roads, and swarmed through the forests.&nbsp;

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:20.320
Even the inhabitants of Winchelsea were in on&nbsp;
the plan. They had fled to Rye under cover of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:27.040
night and fog, taking all their supplies with&nbsp;
them. Louis had walked straight into a trap.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:31.680
William could not confront him head-on,&nbsp;
but it didn’t matter. If the French failed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:36.640
to break through the blockade, they&nbsp;
would simply starve in Winchelsea.&nbsp;

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:41.600
Louis then sent stronger units to try to&nbsp;
break through to London, but the partisans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:47.280
were so dominant in the Weald that they stood&nbsp;
no chance. The biographer of William Marshal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:52.320
Henry’s regent, writes that William&nbsp;
“was not in the mood to play games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.320 --> 00:12:00.560
having many of them beheaded.” Around a thousand&nbsp;
French soldiers never returned from the woods.&nbsp;

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:05.600
Soon, the situation in Winchelsea seemed&nbsp;
hopeless. Hunger and fear drove the French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:11.760
to despair. William’s grip on the forests&nbsp;
was complete—there was no escape. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:16.320
it took a major operation by the French&nbsp;
fleet to save Louis. He escaped across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:23.280
the English Channel at the last moment, and the&nbsp;
partisans melted away into the dense undergrowth.&nbsp;

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:29.120
When Louis returned in April to&nbsp;
continue his invasion of England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:34.960
he saw dark columns of smoke rising above&nbsp;
his siege camp at Dover. A little later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:40.160
a messenger brought him the grim news: William&nbsp;
and his archers had attacked the English garrison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:46.320
overpowered the guards, and set fire to the camp&nbsp;
and the siege engines. Under these circumstances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:52.640
the prince could not risk landing at Dover.
Furious, he ordered his fleet to Sandwich,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:59.280
went ashore, and burned the town to the ground.&nbsp;
This was not simply retaliation—it marked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:05.840
beginning of a new strategy. Louis aimed to&nbsp;
crush the rebels by wiping out their supporters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:12.480
Sandwich was followed in May by Hythe, Romney,&nbsp;
and several small towns near Dover. But this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:18.080
course of action was largely ineffectual. The&nbsp;
edges of the forest had become an increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:24.480
impenetrable barrier for the French, and the&nbsp;
partisans now had reinforcements. Oliver Fitzroy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:29.280
an illegitimate son of John Lackland, had&nbsp;
joined William with some regular troops for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:34.560
the attack on the siege camp, and together,&nbsp;
they worked to keep the French at bay.&nbsp;

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:40.640
However, the stalemate did not last long.&nbsp;
On May 20, William Marshal defeated part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:44.320
of the French forces at the Battle&nbsp;
of Lincoln, and shortly afterward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:49.840
the English fleet delivered the final blow&nbsp;
to the invasion at the Battle of Sandwich.&nbsp;

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:56.400
After the war, William was richly rewarded for his&nbsp;
bravery and loyalty. Henry III granted him lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.400 --> 00:14:01.360
in Essex and appointed him Warden of the Seven&nbsp;
Hundreds of the Weald, making him one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:07.520
important men in the region. According to official&nbsp;
records, William served in this role for 40 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:14.640
before his death in June 1257. He bequeathed his&nbsp;
estate, Cassingham, or Kensham, to his son Ralph.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:21.200
But William’s story did not end with his death.
For more than a century, historians have debated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:26.240
whether the legend of Robin Hood was inspired&nbsp;
by a real historical person, and William is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:31.280
one of the leading candidates. His fight against&nbsp;
the French transformed him from a man of humble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:36.480
origins into a nationally known hero. Even&nbsp;
in the highest circles, people knew about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:42.720
Willikin of the Weald. Historian Sean McGlynn&nbsp;
suggests that his fame was due, at least in part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:48.480
to a royal propaganda campaign. The story of&nbsp;
a common man rising up against the invader was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:54.160
exactly what the royalists needed to inspire&nbsp;
the ordinary people to join the resistance.&nbsp;

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:58.800
Precisely because William’s story was so&nbsp;
well known, McGlynn believes it may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:05.520
inspired—or at least influenced—the legend of&nbsp;
Robin Hood. In fact, there are striking parallels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:10.880
between the two. Like Robin Hood in the earliest&nbsp;
versions of the tale, William was of lower birth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:16.480
branded an outlaw by his enemies, and led a band&nbsp;
of archers fighting in the forests against an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:21.440
oppressor. There’s even a link to Nottingham&nbsp;
and Sherwood Forest, since Nottingham was one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:26.960
of the key royalist strongholds, and William&nbsp;
was almost certainly in contact with them.&nbsp;

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:32.720
As tempting as McGlynn’s theory may be, the name&nbsp;
Robin Hood ultimately became a collective label&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:39.280
for countless robbers and outlaws—a kind of “John&nbsp;
Doe” of medieval bandits. His character likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:45.200
represents the fusion of hundreds of different&nbsp;
tales. The story of William of Kensham, known in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:51.760
his lifetime as the hero of the forest, may well&nbsp;
be one of them. And even if it isn’t, one thing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:57.920
certain: William deserves far more recognition&nbsp;
than history has granted to him so far.&nbsp;

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:02.960
